WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.545
Picture a new employee's first morning. They arrive knowing a

00:00:03.545 --> 00:00:07.090
lot about Minnesota human services. They do not yet know

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.635
who approves an exception in your unit, whether they can

00:00:10.635 --> 00:00:14.179
handle a difficult call, or whether the team will welcome

00:00:14.179 --> 00:00:17.724
them. Those three questions are the core of onboarding. Researchers

00:00:17.724 --> 00:00:20.560
call them role clarity, self-efficacy and social acceptance.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:24.283
When Bauer and colleagues pooled 70 samples of new employees,

00:00:24.283 --> 00:00:27.657
these three explained most of how onboarding connects to later

00:00:27.657 --> 00:00:31.030
satisfaction, commitment, performance and staying. So the test of any

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:34.403
onboarding activity is simple. Does it help the person understand

00:00:34.403 --> 00:00:37.777
the role, feel able to do it, or feel accepted?

00:00:37.777 --> 00:00:41.150
If not, it may be ceremony. Now think about timing.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:45.859
In one study, researchers checked in with 264 new hires

00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:50.218
14 times across their first 90 days. Support from supervisors

00:00:50.218 --> 00:00:54.578
and coworkers dropped over that period. And the early weeks

00:00:54.578 --> 00:00:58.937
mattered most. Early support, or early undermining, predicted later outcomes

00:00:58.937 --> 00:01:02.860
better than any later change. Undermining is often small.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.298
It is a correction in front of others, a key

00:01:06.298 --> 00:01:09.386
email the new person was left off, or a sigh

00:01:09.386 --> 00:01:12.473
when they ask a basic question. Undermining by a supervisor

00:01:12.473 --> 00:01:15.561
was the one behavior uniquely linked to people leaving. That

00:01:15.561 --> 00:01:18.649
means your effort should come early. Before the first day,

00:01:18.649 --> 00:01:20.810
make sure equipment and access will work.

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:24.373
In a study of 985 new hires, a ready workstation

00:01:24.373 --> 00:01:27.586
on Day 1 was linked to better adjustment, and meeting

00:01:27.586 --> 00:01:30.799
the manager on Day 1 was linked to feeling accepted.

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:34.012
Name a peer guide, and give that person real time

00:01:34.012 --> 00:01:37.225
by moving some of their work. Support from experienced colleagues

00:01:37.225 --> 00:01:40.438
is among the strongest tactics in the research. Then ask

00:01:40.438 --> 00:01:41.080
about strengths.

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:45.127
In a field experiment with 605 new call-center agents, a

00:01:45.127 --> 00:01:48.824
one-hour first-day session on each person's best qualities cut turnover.

00:01:48.824 --> 00:01:52.521
Six months later, the standard-training group's turnover was 47.2 percent

00:01:52.521 --> 00:01:56.218
higher. Ask what work the person is proud of, and

00:01:56.218 --> 00:01:59.915
find an early real task that uses it. Finally, put

00:01:59.915 --> 00:02:03.612
check-ins on the calendar now: two weeks, 30 days, 90

00:02:03.612 --> 00:02:05.830
days, six months and one year.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:09.503
At each, ask about the three questions. Good work at

00:02:09.503 --> 00:02:12.826
90 days is encouraging, but it does not mean the

00:02:12.826 --> 00:02:16.149
person feels part of the team. Ask who they can

00:02:16.149 --> 00:02:19.472
go to without hesitation. The evidence here is solid but

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.795
modest. Most studies are correlational, and few come from public

00:02:22.795 --> 00:02:26.119
human services. Good onboarding improves the odds. It does not

00:02:26.119 --> 00:02:27.780
fix a poorly designed job.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:31.117
Watch what happens with each new hire, and improve the

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.610
plan for the next one.
